WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.424
Welcome to CBE STEM Academy, Grade Seven Integrated Science. Today we explore the female reproductive system.

00:00:10.424 --> 00:00:20.000
You will identify its main organs, explain their functions, and follow an egg after ovulation.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:27.460
Most female reproductive organs are inside the pelvis, in the lower part of the abdomen.

00:00:27.460 --> 00:00:40.000
This front-view cutaway lets us see inside. The organs are shown more clearly than they would appear in a living body.

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:47.746
There are two ovaries, one on each side of the uterus. Two fallopian tubes curve above them.

00:00:47.746 --> 00:00:55.707
The uterus lies near the centre. Its narrow lower part is the cervix, which opens into the vagina.

00:00:55.707 --> 00:01:05.000
Follow the labels. The vagina is a canal inside the body; the external genital structures are collectively called the vulva.

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:13.977
The ovaries are small, oval organs. They contain immature egg cells, each surrounded by supporting cells in a follicle.

00:01:13.977 --> 00:01:24.362
From puberty, hormones help follicles develop during reproductive cycles. Usually one egg is released during a cycle in which ovulation occurs.

00:01:24.362 --> 00:01:30.000
The ovaries also produce hormones, including oestrogen and progesterone.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:39.553
The fallopian tubes provide a route towards the uterus. Finger-like ends called fimbriae lie close to each ovary.

00:01:39.553 --> 00:01:50.000
Notice the small gap: the tube is not sealed directly onto the ovary. The fimbriae help guide a released egg into the tube.

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:57.602
The uterus is a hollow, muscular organ. Its inner lining is called the endometrium.

00:01:57.602 --> 00:02:04.688
During a reproductive cycle, this lining can thicken in preparation for a possible pregnancy.

00:02:04.688 --> 00:02:15.000
If pregnancy becomes established, a developing embryo is supported inside the uterus. Identify the wall and the lining separately.

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:24.830
The cervix is the narrow lower part of the uterus. A small cervical canal connects the uterine cavity with the vagina.

00:02:24.830 --> 00:02:30.000
It produces mucus.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:35.873
The vagina is a muscular canal extending from the cervix to the outside of the body.

00:02:35.873 --> 00:02:42.499
Menstrual blood and tissue pass through it. It also forms part of the birth canal.

00:02:42.499 --> 00:02:50.000
Urine leaves through a different opening, the urethra. The vagina is not the urinary passage.

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:58.842
Now watch a simplified close-up of ovulation. A follicle grows as the egg inside it develops.

00:02:58.842 --> 00:03:06.443
When ovulation occurs, the follicle opens and releases the egg from the surface of the ovary.

00:03:06.443 --> 00:03:16.279
The nearby fimbriae help guide it towards the opening of the fallopian tube. An egg does not jump straight into the uterus.

00:03:16.279 --> 00:03:25.000
The egg is enlarged here so you can see it. The movement and timing are slowed or compressed for learning.

00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:33.371
An egg cannot swim along the tube by itself. Tiny hair-like structures called cilia beat along the tube lining.

00:03:33.371 --> 00:03:41.277
Their coordinated movement, together with gentle muscle contractions, helps carry the egg towards the uterus.

00:03:41.277 --> 00:03:51.349
Fertilization, when a sperm joins with an egg, usually happens in a fallopian tube. We will explore that process in a separate lesson.

00:03:51.349 --> 00:04:00.000
If the egg is not fertilized, it eventually breaks down. Movement is enlarged and time is compressed for learning.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:08.473
The ovaries and uterus change together during the menstrual cycle. Hormones help the uterine lining build up.

00:04:08.473 --> 00:04:20.686
If pregnancy does not occur, hormone changes lead to shedding of the lining. Blood and tissue pass through the cervix and vagina. This is menstruation.

00:04:20.686 --> 00:04:30.000
Cycle lengths vary, especially during adolescence. Ovulation does not always happen on the same numbered day.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:36.660
Pause and think: which organs contain developing egg cells? The ovaries.

00:04:36.660 --> 00:04:45.000
Which organ has the lining that thickens and may be shed? The uterus. That lining is the endometrium.

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:51.713
How does an egg move along a fallopian tube? Cilia and gentle muscle contractions help move it.

00:04:51.713 --> 00:05:00.000
Remember: ovaries contain eggs, tubes transport, and the uterus supports pregnancy. Thank you for learning.
